# Database / Supabase safety

Before changing a production RPC:
1. Trace callers
2. Inspect current signature + downstream private functions
3. Check RLS / `security definer` implications
4. Test unauthorized rejection

Prefer unambiguous PL/pgSQL names (`v_`, `p_` prefixes consistent with existing code).  
Avoid ambiguous column/variable collisions (e.g. `grants = grants`).

- Do not expose raw SQL / Postgres error text to players
- Do not weaken RLS for convenience
- Grant/revoke execute deliberately for `authenticated` / public
- Prefer transactional mutations for multi-step inventory/PC changes

## Explicit new-table access (CODEX-43 / October30,2026 change)

New public tables must declare access in the same creating migration; never rely
on Supabase default grants. First revoke all on the specific table from
PUBLIC, anon, authenticated, service_role, then grant only traced operations to
the necessary roles. Enable RLS and scoped policies for player access. RPC-only
storage belongs in private with deliberate function EXECUTE permissions. Do not
copy blanket authenticated CRUD grants from a generic example.

Run node tools/check-new-table-access.js and node tools/validate-build.js.
Historical SQL hashes are evidence snapshots, not a replay-safe baseline; do not
refresh supabase/table-access-history.json to silence a failure. New/changed SQL
needs review. Dynamic DDL, view security, sequence permissions, grant order and
policy correctness also need manual/real-role validation; the source check is
not a complete SQL security parser. Use tools/data-api-access-audit.js for the
read-only live contract check. See docs/project-command-center/DATA-API-OCT30.md.
